    question: 'How do zero-trust VPNs differ from traditional VPNs?',
    answer: '<p>Traditional VPNs use a "castle and moat" approach: once you\'re connected, you have access to the entire network. Zero-trust VPNs verify every access request:</p><ul><li><strong>Least-privilege access:</strong> Users only access specific resources they need</li><li><strong>Continuous verification:</strong> Identity and device health checked throughout sessions</li><li><strong>Micro-segmentation:</strong> Network resources isolated from each other</li><li><strong>No implicit trust:</strong> Even internal users must prove authorization for each resource</li></ul><p>Zero-trust is more secure but requires more setup. For remote teams, it\'s becoming the standard approach as it limits damage from compromised credentials.</p>'
